// aug15b hz0 fuzz divergence_000000 (2026-08-16), the RECURSIVE half
// (the non-recursive half is callee-taint-cache-honor-aug2026).
//
// The interp gives every activation in a recursive call tree its own
// retained lambda instance — measured at ~10.4 KB per call, dead
// linear in the tree (fib(16)/fib(20)/fib(22) = 63/258/634 MB), which
// is Eric's structural ruling 2026-08-13 working as written. So the
// inner `f(n % -1)`, bottoming at the third element, rides ITS OWN
// history (the 0 it computed on the previous element) and `8 - 0`
// publishes.
//
// A kernel's per-call-site block is carved statically out of the
// caller's block, and a self-call cannot do that: the carving would
// nest one level per activation, and the depth is a run-time fact. It
// passed 0 — no memory at all — so the recursive activation's ride
// missed where the interp's rode.
//
// Fix: the self-call roots a lazily-grown TREE of per-activation
// blocks (`graphix_site_child_block`), mirroring the instance tree,
// retained across cycles and freed with the kernel. Keyed by call
// SITE, never by depth — two siblings at the same depth are distinct
// activations with distinct histories. ~82 bytes per activation
// against the interp's ~10.4 KB.
//
// Sharing the caller's own block was tried and REJECTED: aliasing two
// activations' histories produces WRONG VALUES rather than missing
// ones, and here it re-selects the caller's arm and recurses to the
// depth limit.
{let x = array::iter([i64:0, i64:2, i64:0, i64:4]); let rec f = |n: i64| -> i64 select n {i64:0 => i64:0, _ => i64:8 - f(n % i64:-1)}; f(x - i64:-9223372036854775808)}
